window.docContents[39].resolve({"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/#verified-framework":{"contents":"PropertyKindCalculus does no arithmetic of its own. A kinded quantity erases to a magnitude, and\nthe magnitude is computed by a numerical framework: TorchLean\n, whose tensors are generic over\ntheir scalar, whose floating-point formats are specified in Lean (in its FloatLib dependency), and whose CUDA kernels are\ngenerated from Lean source. Write Once, Correctly states the discipline a\nmodel is written in, and the capstone chapter proves what that discipline\nbuys. This chapter states what the discipline demands of the framework underneath it, and what\nthe calculus gives back, in two directions:\n\n* to metrology, a computation that can be accelerated without losing its metrological meaning:\nthe kinds, the declared sources and the listed constants of a model are theorems about the\nprogram that runs, at every carrier, not only about the source it was written in;* to verified numerical frameworks, a consumer whose correctness criterion is bit-for-bit\nagreement across devices, and a set of results that belong to any framework of that kind.\n\nIn plain engineering terms: a unit-and-kind checker usually stops at the source code, and the\nfast version of a model is a second artifact that has to be trusted to match it. Here the fast\nversion is derived from the source, and the derivation carries a proof.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"A Property Kind Calculus for Metrology: proving quantity and unit laws in Lean, from ISO/IEC 80000 to measurement uncertainty","header":"19. Metrology on a Verified Numerical Framework","id":"/Metrology-on-a-Verified-Numerical-Framework/#verified-framework"}});